sections.body:
An **unauthorized commitment** is an agreement that is not binding only because the person who made it lacked authority. Example: a supply sergeant tells a vendor to deliver parts and promises payment.

The fix is **ratification**: an official with authority approves the commitment after the fact (FAR 1.405). Ratification is done by the HCA, and it cannot be delegated below the chief of the contracting office (1.405(b)(2)). It is allowed only when all six criteria in 1.405(c) are met, including:
- the Government received the goods or services;
- the ratifying official has the authority to do it;
- the resulting contract would otherwise have been proper;
- the price is fair and reasonable;
- legal counsel concurs (when agency procedures require it);
- funds were available when the commitment was made.

If ratification is not possible, the vendor may have to seek payment through other legal remedies, and the person who made the promise can face discipline.

Take **FAR 15.404-1(b)**:
- 15 is the **part** (Contracting by Negotiation);
- 15.4 is the **subpart** (Contract Pricing);
- 15.404 is the **section** (Cost and/or price analysis);
- 15.404-1 is the **subsection** (Price analysis);
- (b) is the **paragraph**.

The number after the decimal point starts with the subpart number, then the section within it. Clauses live in Part 52 and carry the number of the part that prescribes them: 52.243-1 is a Changes clause prescribed in Part 43.

You may not obligate or spend more than is available, or obligate before money is available (31 U.S.C. 1341). Violations must be reported, and people involved can be disciplined. That is why:
- a contract is not awarded without a certified purchase request;
- a modification that adds work needs funds before it is signed (FAR 43.205);
- if funds are not yet available, the contract may include 52.232-18, Availability of Funds, so the Government is not liable until funds are.
